# Final Planned vs Actual

The Awtsmoos turns plan into vessel, then tests the vessel against the sea;
Awtsmoos.com reached a smaller, faster crawl design than the first map imagined to be.

## Planned

- give Google a root sitemap door;
- make Heichel and Road content visible without client JavaScript;
- generate canonical post metadata;
- expose crawlable hierarchy links;
- ship the result through canonical `main` and production release;
- preserve concurrent local work.

## Actual

All planned outcomes were achieved, with two material architecture improvements discovered during execution.

### Delta 1 — deep sitemap recursion rejected

The first deep per-Heichel sitemap implementation was measured at 30–120+ seconds and could monopolize a verifier. It was removed completely.

The final architecture is faster and more standards-native:

`robots.txt → sitemap seeds → public Heichel HTML → series HTML → stable named-post HTML`.

Ordinary `<a href>` links carry depth; the sitemap seeds discovery instead of rebuilding the whole Torah graph synchronously.

### Delta 2 — release verifier repaired

Production activation revealed a separate tunnel-agent manifest defect. Rather than bypassing the release guard, the authoritative manifest was regenerated and its dedicated release suite was passed before production was allowed to switch.

## Verification actually completed

- source syntax/import/XML checks;
- Heichel quality suite over 1,261 files;
- 135-route API contract suite;
- release ZIP/runtime probe;
- 23/23 tunnel-release tests;
- isolated/local real-data raw HTTP acceptance;
- direct canonical public HTTPS acceptance;
- numeric-vs-named Torah body identity;
- missing-post noindex proof;
- browser navigation shadow proof;
- final local-main/origin-main equality;
- test-server cleanup.

## Remaining delta

No implementation, release, verification, or repository-reconciliation delta remains for this mission.

Google and other search engines still control their own crawl scheduling, indexing decisions, and cache refresh timing. That is external system latency, not unfinished repository work.
